TI  - LLM-Generated Heuristics for Guiding Reinforcement Learning in Autonomous Cyber Defense
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 290
SP  - 226
EP  - 233
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.08.388
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052809635?origin=resultslist
AB  - Reinforcement learning has been successfully applied to autonomous cyber defense, but it suffers from high sample complexity which hinders its adaptability to new environments and threats. LLM-based teacher-guided techniques have been used to mitigate these issues, but LLMs have a high inference cost and use a great deal of computational resources, making them difficult to apply in real-world autonomous cyber defense scenarios. In this paper, we introduce a novel LLM-based action masking strategy for autonomous cyber defense where an LLM produces a collection of heuristics to guide reinforcement learning. The LLM generated heuristics are implemented as action masks throughout the training process as a means of providing guidance to the RL agent in order to mitigate poor initial performance. We distinguish between recommendation heuristics, which specify actions that should be taken in a specific scenario, and constraint heuristics, which specify actions that should not be taken in a specific scenario. We apply action masks according to these heuristics in two ways: 1) only allowing recommended actions to be selected, and 2) disallowing actions based on constraint heuristics. We compare our LLM enhanced reinforcement learning agent to a baseline reinforcement learning agent with no LLM guidance in the CybORG++ reinforcement learning environment. We evaluate training convergence and performance across 10 independent runs and analyze how the heuristic-based action masks influence action selection.  © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.
