TI  - The AI Agent Who Cried Wolf: Boundary-Oriented Threat Models for Model Context Protocol (MCP)
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Communications Magazine
DO  - 10.1109/MCOM.001.2600567
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053976293?origin=resultslist
AB  - Model Context Protocol (MCP) is rapidly emerging as a core interface that lets Large Language Model (LLM) applications interact with external tools, resources, and real-world services. Yet in MCPbased systems, user intent, accepted capability, and external execution can diverge across separate stages. We map this divergence to three boundary-specific threat models: T1 Meaning-in-the-Middle for Host-side interpretation of user intent and approval context, T2 Trojan Capability for the acceptance of server-supplied capability semantics, and T3 Hidden Execution for externally realized side effects. For each threat model, we describe exposed assets, attacker insertion points, manipulation strategies, and the paths by which compromise propagates into later stages. We further use an implemented shopping-agent case and an AI-assisted telecom security-operations scenario to show how boundary failures produce hidden economic and network-operational effects. Finally, we derive architectural requirements for secure MCP systems, organize them around context verification, explicit capability acceptance, and State-Locked Execution, and illustrate them through a structured architecture. © 1979-2012 IEEE.
