TI  - Explaining Hybrid RL Agents for Cyber Defense using SHAP and Reward Decomposition
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - IEEE Military Communications Conference MILCOM
SP  - 1602
EP  - 1607
DO  - 10.1109/MILCOM64451.2025.11310039
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031780485?origin=resultslist
AB  - In high-stakes domains such as cybersecurity for military networks, the ability to interpret and trust the behavior of autonomous agents is essential. In this work, we apply post hoc explainability methods to a Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)-based cyber defense agent developed for the CAGE Challenge 2. The agent employs a hybrid architecture that integrates DRL, rule-based systems, and Large Language Models (LLMs). We analyze the agent's decision-making using two complementary explainability techniques: SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and reward decomposition. SHAP highlights which input features most influence the agent's actions, while reward decomposition attributes the agent's rewards to semantically meaningful environmental events. Our results show that the agent prioritizes clean, low-risk host states and exhibits a conservative strategy shaped by penalties associated with administrator access and restore actions. Furthermore, we propose possible enhancements such as LLM-generated textual explanations and formal verification to improve trust, transparency, and usability. This work lays the foundation for extending explainability methods to multi-agent reinforcement learning settings, with a focus on future Software-Defined Defense (SDD) scenarios.  © 2025 IEEE.
