TI  - Towards generic defensive cyber agents: Leveraging large language models and RAG-based few-shot learning
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computer Networks
VL  - 285
C7  - 112349
DO  - 10.1016/j.comnet.2026.112349
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038455726?origin=resultslist
AB  - The dynamic and evolving nature of cyber threats requires defensive mechanisms that can adapt to new adversaries and network environments without the high cost of retraining. This work introduces a generic cyber defense framework that integrates reinforcement learning (RL), large language models (LLMs), retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and few-shot learning to achieve scalable and adaptive defense. The framework leverages a data store of observation–action tuples collected from RL agents trained against specific adversaries. At inference, RAG retrieves the top-K relevant tuples for the current observation, which are incorporated into an LLM prompt to enable few-shot reasoning. The LLM then predicts the most suitable defensive action for the given observation, with adaptability to previously unseen adversaries and network topologies achieved simply by augmenting the data store with new observation–action tuples rather than retraining new agents. Unlike state-of-the-art hierarchical or RL–supervised hybrid approaches, the proposed framework does not require additional agent training when new attack strategies emerge, ensuring both adaptability and scalability. Extensive evaluation across various adversarial scenarios demonstrates that medium-sized models, particularly Mistral-7B and Gemma-7B when combined with the proposed framework deliver the most effective defensive actions. Comparative analysis further shows that our framework consistently outperforms existing methods in defensive success rates while uniquely enabling rapid extension to new adversaries. These results establish the proposed approach as a robust and scalable pathway for advancing autonomous cyber defense systems. The dynamic and evolving nature of cyber threats requires defensive mechanisms that can adapt to new adversaries and network configurations without the high cost of retraining. This work introduces a generic cyber defense framework that integrates reinforcement learning (RL), large language models (LLMs), retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and few-shot learning to achieve scalable and adaptive defense. The framework leverages a datastore of observation–action tuples collected from RL agents trained against specific adversaries. At inference, RAG retrieves the top-K relevant tuples for the current observation, which are incorporated into an LLM prompt to enable few-shot reasoning. The LLM then predicts the most suitable defensive action for the given observation, with adaptation to new adversarial behaviors and topology variations achieved by augmenting the datastore with additional observation–action tuples rather than retraining the generic defensive agent. Unlike state-of-the-art hierarchical or RL–supervised hybrid approaches, the proposed framework decouples adaptation from retraining of the deployed defender, enabling scalable incorporation of emerging threats. Evaluation across multiple adversarial scenarios and topology variants within the CybORG environment demonstrates that medium-sized models, particularly Mistral-7B and Gemma-7B, when combined with the proposed framework, deliver the most effective defensive actions. Comparative analysis further shows that our framework consistently outperforms existing methods in defensive success rates while enabling rapid extension to additional adversaries. These results establish the proposed approach as a scalable and practically deployable pathway for advancing autonomous cyber defense systems. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
