TI  - Building an Autonomous Cyber Defense System Through Generative AI Framework
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on AI Innovations and Industry, ICAIII 2026 - Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/ICAIII69475.2026.11521460
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041472079?origin=resultslist
AB  - This research develops and evaluates the D-GEN-SOC, a Security Operation Center (SOC) framework that is defensively oriented and combines open-source telemetry (Wazuh), incident coordination (The Hive), and a lightweight AI augmentation grounded in explainable automation. The project prototypes a modular architecture that is designed for transparent, ethical, and auditable SOC decision support. This study adopted a Design Science Research (DSR) approach to design, implement and evaluate the D-GEN-SOC framework. A controlled lab environment was deployed on an Ubuntu-based system to simulate realistic activity and generate attack that were synthetic, such as SSH brute-force attempts. These performances were consumed by Wazuh, analysed, and automatically forwarded to TheHive using a Python-based connector. A local AI module that summarizes those alerts using Gemini 1.5's free-tier API, which creates a small summary report that is stored alongside for analyst and human-in-the-loop (HITL) validation. The assessment scripts measured the Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) and alert-forwarding latency across 20 alerts, and a prototype was achieved with an MTTD of 84.83s and a Wazuh → TheHive latency of 29.55 s, demonstrating a near-real-time detection and a reliable coordination between the SIEM and SOAR layers. This visualization also includes a timeline graph and a histogram that reveals a stable sub-minute response cycle. During the experiments, the Cortex analyser layer was unstable, and the results confirmed that even partial AI augmentation, log summarization, prioritization, and metrics computation can improve the explainability and readiness for analyst without replacing the human authority. D-GEN-SOC offers a culturally aligned blueprint for NZ organizations to integrate AI within open-source SOC ecosystems. The findings also demonstrated the measures that improved MTTD by ∼53%), MTTR by ( 4 0 %), and fewer false-positive reductions by (10 %), validating the framework' s operational feasibility. © 2026 IEEE.
